<Article
  locale="en"
  page="privacy"
  title="Privacy | HEIC to JPG"
  description="Your photos are converted inside your browser and are never uploaded. Here is exactly what this site does and does not collect, and how to check it yourself."
  h1="Privacy"
  updated="Last updated 29 August 2026"
>
  <p>
    This site converts your photos without ever receiving them. That is the whole
    privacy policy. The rest of this page explains how that works and how to check
    it, because a claim like that is worth nothing if you have to take it on faith.
  </p>

  <h2>Your photos</h2>
  <p>
    When you choose or drop a HEIC file, the browser hands the file to code
    running on this page. That code decodes it using a WebAssembly build of
    libheif and re-encodes it as a JPG, all inside the tab. No request carrying
    your photo is ever made. There is no server that could receive one.
  </p>
  <p>
    We therefore never see, store, transmit or delete your photos, because we
    never had them. Closing the tab is all the deletion there is.
  </p>

  <h2>How to check that yourself</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Open your browser's developer tools and select the Network tab.</li>
    <li>Convert a photo on the home page.</li>
    <li>
      Look at every request that appears. You will see the page, its stylesheet,
      its script, the <code>.wasm</code> decoder, and <code>blob:</code> entries,
      which are your own browser's references to files it holds in memory. There
      is no upload.
    </li>
    <li>
      For a stricter test, disconnect from the internet after the page has loaded
      and convert a photo. It still works.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we do collect</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing that identifies you. There are no accounts, no cookies set by this
    site, no advertising, no third-party trackers and no fingerprinting. We do not
    know how many photos you converted or what was in them.
  </p>

  <h2>Server logs</h2>
  <p>
    The site is served by Cloudflare, which records standard request logs for the
    pages and files it serves, including IP address, user agent and the URL
    requested. These are the logs any web server keeps in order to work. They
    never contain your photos, because your photos are never requested from a
    server.
  </p>

  <h2>Links away from here</h2>
  <p>
    This site is made by <a href="https://fotofoto.app" rel="noopener">foto foto</a>,
    which prints photo books. If you follow a link to it you are on a different
    site with its own privacy policy. Nothing is carried across: your photos stay
    in this tab, and foto foto receives no data about what you converted.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about any of this can go to
    <a href="mailto:hello@fotofoto.app">hello@fotofoto.app</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
